The scoring key accepts 165. Continuing the tripled steps (2, 6, 18, then 54) gives 174 − 54 = 120, and 101 respondents typed 120, against 13 who typed 165. I can't find the rule that leads to 165; the key may encode a pattern I haven't seen, or record a slip. Scored with 120, NS_6 would be solved by 11.5% of those who answered: still the hardest item, but at about half the rate of NS_7 and NS_9 (0.24) rather than a sixteenth. Either way, its place in the data is mostly a fact about its key, not the construct. The map is what made the item look wrong.

My working rule: when an item lands far from where the construct map puts it, I read the item and its key before I revise the map. [AI and psychometrics](ai-psychometrics.qmd) returns to these nine items as a check on its own pipeline.

